<p>image</p>
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Types of Assembly" id="Types of Assembly">
<Enumerate>

    <Item><strong>Manual Bench Assembly:</strong> Human operators assembling on a work bench or a fixed location transferring parts to their space to complete assemblies.</Item>
    <Item><strong>Manual Assembly Line:</strong> Human operators assembling parts on a moving line passing their work down to the next assembly step human operator.</Item>
    <Item><strong>Robotic Assembly:</strong> Robotic devices in the place of humans, completing part or the entirety of the assembly process.</Item>
    <Item><strong>Special Purpose Transfer Machine Assembly:</strong> Fully automated machines designed specifically assemble and create a mass-produced product</Item>

</Enumerate>
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Assembly Operations" id="Assembly Operations">
<p>In order for one to calculate overall time for manufacturing, they must consider all types of assembly operations. These operations are the following.</p>
<Itemize>

    <Item><strong>Handling <InlineEquation equation="\\&" /> Alignment Time varies by:</strong></Item>
    <Itemize>
        <Item>How is this part taken a hold of? Does it get moved by tweezers, a specific tool, hands, or multiple people?</Item>
        <Item>How does this part present itself? It could be in a pile of parts, conveyor, dispenser etc.</Item>
        <Item>What is the size of the object? If the object is small or large, it can take more time to assemble than the medium/ideal size to assemble.</Item>
        <Item>Is the part symmetrical? Is orientation or rotation necessary for alignment</Item>
    </Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Insertion Time varies by:</strong></Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item>Insertion tolerance <InlineEquation equation="\\&" /> hole size</Item>
            <Item>If access to hole or view is obstructed</Item>
            <Item>Necessary force for insertion or depth</Item>
            <Item>Additional support while inserting the component</Item>
        </Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Secure Time Varies By:</strong></Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item>If part threaded, a bolt or screw?</Item>
            <Item>Snap fit or action</Item>
            <Item>Riveted (solid, tubular, blind, or two part)</Item>
            <Item>Press Fit</Item>
            <Item>Crimp</Item>

    </Itemize>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Assembly Time:" id="Assembly Time:">
<p>Now that we have covered all of the topics of design for assembly, how can we now consider the time for assembly? We can consider additional time increments for assembly in the following ways:</p>
<Enumerate>

    <Item><strong>Part Symmetry:</strong> When considering assembly time for inserting parts, we must consider <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}" />, the rotational symmetry about an axis perpendicular to the axis of insertion. We must consider <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{\\beta}" />, the rotational symmetry about the axis of insertion. The angles of <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}" /> and <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{\\beta}" /> are the smallest which the part can be rotated and repeat its orientation for insertion.</Item>
        <DisplayEquation equation='ST = \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{360}' title="" />
    <Item><strong>Part Handling - Aspect Ratio:</strong> When considering length, <InlineEquation equation="<strong>L}" />, of the smallest rectangular prism that can enclose the part. Then we must consider the thickness, <InlineEquation equation="<strong>t}" /> of the part. We can also make acceptations for rods, shafts, or pin like parts and use radius <InlineEquation equation="<strong>r}" />. Using these standards, we can now define <strong>Aspect Ratio</strong> as the ratio between the longest and shortest lengths of the rectangular prism. They are defined as the following:</Item>
        <DisplayEquation equation='AR = \\frac{L}{t}' title="" /> or <DisplayEquation equation='AR = \\frac{L}{r}' title="" />
<Item><strong>Part Handling Difficulties:</strong> We can ask the following questions for the part:</Item>
    <Itemize>

        <Item><strong>Slippery:</strong> Easily slips from grip</Item>
        <Item><strong>Fragile:</strong> Requires careful handling</Item>
        <Item><strong>Sharp:</strong> Presents hazards to operator</Item>
        <Item><strong>Stick Together:</strong> Magnetic Force or Grease </Item>
        <Item><strong>Nest or Tangle:</strong> Can be separated with one hand</Item>
        <Item><strong>Severe Nest or Tangle:</strong> Needs two hands to separate</Item>
        <Item><strong>Flexible Parts:</strong> Needs two hands to manipulate and assemble</Item>

</Itemize>

</Enumerate>
</SubSubSection>
<SubSubSubSection title="Time Approximations" id="Time Approximations">
<p>Since we have defined our part sizes and degrees between possible orientation, we can now make the following estimates</p>
<p><u><strong>Handling <InlineEquation equation="\\&" /> Alignment Time</u></strong></p>
<Enumerate>

    <Item><strong>Retrieve Part:</strong> 0.5s per 0.5 m of distance (0.5 m minimum)</Item>
    <Item><strong>Symmetry Factor:</strong> add <DisplayEquation equation='ST = \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{360}' title="" /> </Item>
    <Item><strong>Part Size Factor:</strong></Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item>If Small where <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{L}" /> <InlineEquation equation="\\textless" /> 2 cm, add 0.5s</Item>
            <Item>Large part if <InlineEquation equation="\\boldsymbol{L}" /> <InlineEquation equation="\\textgreater" /> 20 cm, add 0.3s</Item>

</Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Handling Difficulty:</strong> For each handling difficulty where the part is sharp, tangle-prone, flexible, etc., add 0.4 s</Item>
    <Item><strong>Aspect Ratio Factor:</strong> If <strong>AR</strong> <InlineEquation equation="\\textgreater" /> 20, add 0.1s. If <strong>AR</strong> <InlineEquation equation="\\textgreater" /> 40, add 0.3s</Item>

</Enumerate>
<p><u><strong>Insert <InlineEquation equation="\\&" /> Secure Time</u></strong></p>
<Enumerate>

    <Item><strong>General Placement:</strong> Add 0.5s</Item>
    <Item><strong>Hole Alignment:</strong></Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item>If small hole diameter <InlineEquation equation="\\textless" /> 2 mm, add 0.7s. </Item>
            <Item>If medium hole diameter between 2 mm <InlineEquation equation="\\textless" /> diameter <InlineEquation equation="\\textless" /> 4 mm, add 0.1s.</Item>

</Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Pin Alignment:</strong> Opposite Alignment for hole.</Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item>if small diameter <InlineEquation equation="<" /> 20 mm, add 0.4s</Item>
            <Item>if medium diameter between 2 mm <InlineEquation equation="<" /> diameter <InlineEquation equation="<" /> 4 mm, add 0.1s.</Item>

</Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Using Grasping Assistance:</strong> If needs a tool assistance to move, add 1.4s</Item>
    <Item><strong>Turning Insertion:</strong> Starting a screw, nut or bolt, add 1s.</Item>
    <Item><strong>Fitting:</strong> for the following types</Item>
        <Itemize>

            <Item><strong>Crimp:</strong> Add 0.8s</Item>
            <Item><strong>Snap:</strong> Add 0.3s</Item>

</Itemize>
    <Item><strong>Final Tightening of Screw/Nut:</strong> For tightening screw or nut, add 2 s if one sided. If two sided, add 7 s.</Item>
    <Item><strong>Insertion Difficulty:</strong> For difficulties such as view, force, spring, holding and moving, tight tolerances, etc., add 0.4 s for each</Item>
    <Item><strong>Rotate Base:</strong> For every time turning assembly over, add 1.8 s</Item>

</Enumerate>
</SubSubSubSection>
<SubSubSection title="Assembly Efficiency" id="Assembly Efficiency">
<p>Given by the equation:</p>
<DisplayEquation equation='AE = \\frac{Nt}{t_{tot}}' title="" />
<p>Is in terms of:</p>
    <Itemize>

        <Item><strong>N</strong> is the minimum number of parts needed in the assembly</Item>
        <Item><strong>t</strong> is the <em>ideal</em> assembly time, for small parts that present no difficulties in handling, orientation, or assembly. <strong>t</strong> is normally <strong>3 seconds</strong>.</Item>
        <Item>{<InlineEquation equation="t_{tot}" />} is the <strong><em>total</strong></em> assembly time</Item>
        <Item>Products with 5-10$\</Item>
        <Item>Well designed parts have efficiencies around 25$\</Item>
        <Item>Assembly efficiencies around 100$\</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>
